From d21a81de80830113a38edd03fd6bc23b3a08488e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:01:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/10] feat(control-plane): add a vLLM process launcher

The fake launcher proves the loop, but a real deployment needs an
instance that serves a model. VLLMProcessLauncher runs every instance
as a child process of the control plane:

  <command> --model=<spec.model.reference> --host <bind> --port <free>
            --tensor-parallel-size <device count>

The command defaults to the control plane's interpreter with
`-m vllm.entrypoints.openai.api_server` and can be replaced (another
virtualenv, or a stand-in in tests). The model is passed as
`--model=<ref>` so a reference starting with "-" cannot become an
option. CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES comes from the allocated devices: an
inventory device id must be a CUDA index (`0`, `gpu-0`) or a GPU-/MIG-
UUID; CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER defaults to PCI_BUS_ID so indexes match
nvidia-smi. FORGEMESH_* variables are not passed to instances.

Instances can only run on the host of the control plane, so the
launcher is bound to one inventory node and refuses devices of other
nodes, runtimes other than `vllm` and blank model references before
starting anything.

Readiness: verify polls GET /health until it answers 200, and fails as
soon as the process exits or the ready timeout (measured from the
process start, so retried verify attempts fail at once) has passed.
Stop: every instance runs in its own session; stop sends SIGTERM to the
process group, then SIGKILL after the grace period, and SIGKILL to the
group in any case because vLLM workers can outlive the leader. aclose
stops all instances; after a restart the launcher knows no instance.

The tests drive a stand-in executable that mimics the vLLM command
line and /health, with modes for slow start, early exit, hanging,
ignoring SIGTERM and a child process that ignores SIGTERM.
